<Base title="About - Morgan Travis">
  <div class="about-header">
    <h1>About</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="about-section">
    <h2>Official Bio</h2>
    <p>Morgan Travis is a customer operations and support leader with a decade of experience building and scaling customer-facing teams at AWS, 4 Optics, and across government contracting and enterprise environments. As a Senior Manager at AWS Enterprise Support, she oversaw operations serving 400 to 500 enterprise customers across government, financial services, and healthcare - managing technical account managers, escalation workflows, and the systems that kept them running.</p>
    <p>Her work focuses on applying DevOps and lean rigor to support operations: building measurement frameworks, coaching infrastructure, and the operating models that let teams deliver consistently at scale. She has led AI readiness assessments, built automation pipelines for enterprise support workflows, and developed the Support Operations framework - a direct translation of the DORA software delivery research for customer-facing teams.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="about-section">
    <h2>Personal Bio</h2>
    <p>I am an operations builder. I get dropped into situations where a team cares deeply but the systems have not caught up - and I build the infrastructure to match the intention.</p>
    <p>I have done this in enterprise cloud support, in digital consulting, in government contracting, and in civil engineering finance. The problems look different. The pattern is the same: good people, unclear measurement, no operating model, and a belief that if you just hire more people the quality will improve.</p>
    <p>It does not. The system does.</p>
    <p>I write about Support Operations - the application of DevOps rigor to customer-facing teams. Nobody has published a coherent framework for this. I am writing it.</p>
  </div>

